NAME
    vi - a minimal screen editor

SYNOPSIS
    vi name

DESCRIPTION
    A modal, vi-flavoured screen editor for a VT100/ANSI terminal on the
    serial console (it positions the cursor with ANSI escapes and reads
    keys raw, without echo). The file is a flat buffer of fixed-width
    lines: up to 320 lines of up to 79 characters. A file that does not
    exist starts as an empty buffer ("[new file]"). Longer lines, or
    lines past the 320th, are cut when the file is read; the status row
    then says [cut], and writing the buffer back writes the cut text.
    Files are written with LF line ends. vi is /BIN/VI.

    The screen is 24 x 80: 23 text rows (~ marks rows past the end of
    the file) and a status row showing the mode, the file name, [+] when
    there are unsaved changes, and one-off messages.

NORMAL MODE
        h j k l    move left / down / up / right (the arrow keys too)
        0  $       start / end of line
        G          last line
        i a A      insert before / after the cursor / at end of line
        o          open a new line below and insert there
        x          delete the character under the cursor
        dd         delete the line
        u          undo the last change (one level: x, dd, o, or the
                   text typed in one insert on one line)
        /pat       search forward for the literal text pat (wraps once);
                   an empty pattern repeats the last one
        n          repeat the search
        Ctrl-L     repaint the screen

    Commands (type : then the command and Enter; Esc cancels):
        :w         write the file
        :w name    write the buffer to another file (the buffer keeps
                   its own name, and [+] stays)
        :wq  :x    write and quit (either may name a file: :x name)
        :q         quit (refused while there are unsaved changes)
        :q!        quit without saving

INSERT MODE
        Printable characters are inserted; Enter splits the line;
        Backspace or DEL deletes to the left; Esc returns to NORMAL.
        A line stops growing at 78 characters.

END OF INPUT
    Ctrl-D (end of console input) acts as Esc in INSERT mode and as :q
    in NORMAL mode. Sixteen in a row abandon the edit without saving;
    that only happens when the input is gone for good (a scripted run
    on the emulators).

    On the instruction-level emulator a q byte is read as end of input
    (docs/programming/EMULATORS.md), so :q, :wq and text containing q
    cannot be typed there: use :x to save and quit. The microcode
    emulator and the machine have no such quirk.

EXAMPLES
    vi notes.txt
    vi /README.TXT        then  jo  some text  Esc  :x /T.TXT

